# The upstream harness version this tree was taken from. One line, first
# non-comment: a release NUMBER, monotonically increasing, with the real
# calendar date of that release beside it in brackets.
#
# It was specified as an ISO date, and that was wrong within two days. Bumping a
# date per round drifts it off the calendar the moment two rounds land on one
# day: nine releases went out between 2026-09-11 and 2026-09-13 and were stamped
# 2026-09-11 through 2026-09-19. Six of those dates were claims about when that
# were simply false, and the drift could not be corrected downward without
# making a vendored copy look NEWER than the harness it came from.
#
# A number cannot drift. The date in brackets is the honest one, and is there
# because "which release is this" and "how old is it" are different questions and
# a reader usually wants both. Nothing parses either: check-boundaries.sh asks
# only whether this file CHANGED, and doctor.sh prints it.
#
# It exists because a vendored copy cannot be dated from the outside: it carries
# the consuming project's git history, not this repository's. Two field reports
# in a row arrived reporting defects that had been fixed upstream for weeks, and
# neither could say which harness it had measured - so every finding had to be
# re-verified by hand before it could be called already-fixed.
#
#   bash scripts/doctor.sh          prints it, so it lands in every environment
#                                   record and every story that quotes one
#   scripts/check-boundaries.sh     refuses an upstream harness change that does
#                                   not bump it
#
# A field report against this harness states the version it measured.
#
# TWO older shapes a vendored copy may still hold, both meaning "older than
# every number here":
#   * a bare date, e.g. `2026-09-16` - stamped, but from before the numbering;
#   * no file at all - from before stamping entirely.
81 (2026-10-06)
